## ABSTRACT
In this study, we developed a novel confinement-synthesis approach to layered double hydroxide nanodots (LDH-NDs) anchored on carbon nanoparticles, which formed a three-dimensional (3D) interconnected network within a porous carbon support derived from pyrolysis of metal-organic frameworks (C-MOF). The resultant LDH-NDs $@$ C-MOF nonprecious metal catalysts were demonstrated to exhibit super-high catalytic performance for oxygen evolution reaction (OER) with excellent operation stability and low overpotential $(\sim230\:\mathsf{m V})$ at an exchange current density of $10\mathsf{m A}{\cdot}\mathsf{c m}^{-2}$ . The observed overpotential for the LDH-NDs $@$ C-MOF is much lower than that of large-sized LDH nanosheets $(321~\mathsf{m V})$ , pure carbonized MOF $(411~\mathsf{m V})$ , and even commercial ${\mathsf{R u O}}_{2}$ $(281~\mathsf{m V})$ . X-ray absorption measurements and density functional theory (DFT) calculations revealed partial charge transfer from $\mathsf{F e}^{3+}$ through an O bridge to ${\mathsf{N i}}^{2+}$ at the edge of LDH-NDs supported by C-MOF to produce the optimal binding energies for OER intermediates. This, coupled with a large number of exposed active sides and efficient charge and electrolyte/reactant/product transports associated with the porous 3D C-MOF support, significantly boosted the OER performance of the LDH-ND catalyst with respect to its nanosheet counterpart. Apart from the fact that this is the first active side identification for LDH-ND OER catalysts, this work provides a general strategy to enhance activities of nanosheet catalysts by converting them into edge-rich nanodots to be supported by 3D porous carbon architectures.
## KEYWORDS
carbon nanomaterials, layered double hydroxide (LDH) nanodots, metal-organic framework (MOF) derivatives, oxygen evolution reaction
## Introduction
Electrocatalytic oxygen evolution reaction (OER) is a key for clean and renewable energy conversion technologies, including rechargeable metal-air batteries and water splitting [1–4]. Although certain noble metal-based catalysts (e.g., $\mathrm{RuO}_{2}$ , $\mathrm{IrO}_{2}^{'}$ ) have been demonstrated to show the best activity for OER, their large-scale application has been seriously impeded by the high cost and poor stability of noble metal catalysts. As promising alternatives, various nonprecious metal catalysts (NPMCs), such as traditional transition metal oxides [5, 6], perovskites [7–10], phosphides [11–15] and two-dimensional (2D) layered double hydroxides (LDHs) [16–18], have been developed to replace commercial noble metal-based OER catalysts. For this purpose, several innovative strategies, including hybridization [19, 20], heteroatom doping [21–23], and exfoliation [24–26], have been recently devised to reduce the energy barriers and increase the exposed number of the active sites for OER by turning the atomic/electronic structures of NPMCs.
Of particular interest, ultrathin LDH nanosheets (NSs) with an atomic thickness were prepared by chemical exfoliation to increase the number of exposed active sites, and hence the improved OER performance [24, 27]. Furthermore, LDH-based hybrid catalysts (e.g., LDHs/CNT, LDHs/graphene) with an enhanced conductivity and interfacial interaction to facilitate the electron transfer and absorption of oxygen-containing reactants have also been reported [17, 19, 28]. However, almost all of the previously-reported LDH-based catalysts are large-sized nanosheets with a relatively small portion of the edge sites for effective OER electrocatalysis [27]. To optimize the OER performance, therefore, it is highly desirable, though challenging, to develop a class of new LDH hybrid catalysts with an excellent conductivity, a high surface area, and abundant effective (edge) active sites.
As is well known, three-dimensional (3D)/2D nanodots (NDs) possess a significantly higher edge/volume ratio than that of large-size 2D NSs. Thus, one of the attractive options towards high-performance electrocatalysts is to convert 2D NSs into the edge-rich 3D NDs with desired atomic/electronic and materials structures. To enhance the relatively low conductivity intrinsically associated with 3D NDs, 3D carbon electrodes with an interconnected open porosity, such as graphene foams, metalorganic frameworks (MOFs), and their derivatives [29–31], can be used as ideal supports for the LDH-NDs catalysts to provide multiple advantages, including a large surface area for maximized exposure of the active sites, 3D conductive pathway for efficient electron transport, and tunable porosity for facilitated electrolyte diffusion.
## 2 Experimental
## 2.1    Synthesis of C‐MOF
In a typical experiment, $450.0\ \mathrm{mg\Co(NO_{3})_{2}.6H_{2}O}$ was added into $3~\mathrm{mL}$ ultrapure water to produce solution A. Then, $5.5\ \mathrm{g}$ 2-methylimidazole was dissolved completely in $20~\mathrm{mL}$ ultrapure water to obtain solution B. Thereafter, solution A was poured into solution B under stirring $(800~\mathrm{rpm})$ for 5 min and subsequently kept at room temperature for $6\mathrm{{h}}$ . The resultant purple product was washed with anhydrous methanol for 3–5 times, followed by drying in a vacuum oven for overnight. The asprepared zeolitic imidazolate framework-67 (ZIF-67) was calcined at $700^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $1.5\mathrm{h}$ under Ar atmosphere to produce the C-MOF. Finally, the C-MOF thus produced was immersed in $2\mathrm{M}$ concentrated HCl solution for $^{8\mathrm{h}}$ to remove the free cobalt particles.
## 2.2    Synthesis of LDH‐NDs@C‐MOF and LDH‐NSs
$50.0~\mathrm{\mg}$ C-MOF was firstly dispersed into $40.0\ \mathrm{\mL}$ Nmethylpyrrolidone (NMP) by ultrasonication (Bandelin, Sonorex) for $30\ \mathrm{min}$ . $\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{3}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ $(735.0~\mathrm{mg})$ and $\mathrm{Fe}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{3}{\cdot}9\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ $(340.0~\mathrm{mg})$ were then dissolved in Mill-Q water $(50.0~\mathrm{mL}$ ), and mixed with the C-MOF-NMP dispersion and kept at $100^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $2.5\mathrm{h}$ without stirring. Then, the as-prepared LDH- ${\mathrm{ND}}s@{\mathrm{C}}-$ MOF was washed with Mill-Q water by cnetrifugation to neutral, followed by freeze drying. LDH-NSs was prepared by the same synthetic process as that for the LDH-NDs@C-MOF without the addition of the C-MOF.
## 2.3 $x$ ‐ray  absorption  data  collection,  analysis  and modelling
## 2.3.1 Characterization
Transmission electron microscopy (TEM) and high-resolution TEM (HRTEM) were performed on a FEI Tecnai G2 F20 electron microscope operated at 200 and $80~\mathrm{kV},$ respectively. Scanning electron microscopy (SEM) images were recorded using a Hitachi S4800 field emission scanning electron microscope at $6~\mathrm{kV}$ with the software package for automated electron tomography. Powder X-ray diffraction (PXRD) analysis was performed on the X’Pert PRO X-ray diffractometer using Cu $\mathrm{K}\alpha$ radiation over the $2\theta$ range from $5^{\circ}$ to $90^{\circ}$ at a scan rate of $3^{\mathrm{o}}$ per min. X-ray photoelectron spectroscopic (XPS) analyses were performed on a Thermo Scientific, UK (model ESCALAB250Xi).
## 2.3.2 Electrochemical measurements
The catalyst dispersion or ink was prepared using mixture of $0.2~\mathrm{mL}$ water, $0.3~\mathrm{mL}$ ethanol, $0.02\mathrm{~mL}5\mathrm{~wt.\%}$ Nafion solution and $2~\mathrm{mg}$ catalysts followed by ultrasonication for $^{2\mathrm{~h~}}$ . Then, $10\upmu\mathrm{L}$ of the ink was uniformly loaded onto a freshly polished glassy-carbon electrode (diameter $=0.5~\mathrm{cm}$ ), which was used as the working electrode with a catalyst loading of $0.2~\mathrm{mg}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ The electrochemical tests were performed in a three-electrode electrochemical cell (Pine Instruments) using a $\mathrm{Pt}$ wire and an $\mathrm{\Ag/AgCl}$ electrode as counter electrode and reference electrode, respectively. To make our tests rigorous, high-purity KOH $(99.98\%)$ was used to prepare the $1\mathrm{~M~}$ electrolyte in a polypropylene bottle. Before detecting $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ species, high-purity $\mathrm{N}_{2}$ gas was bubbled through the solution for at least $30~\mathrm{min}$ to remove the dissolved $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ from air. All potentials measured were calibrated to a standard reversible hydrogen electrode (RHE) using the following equation: $E_{\mathrm{RHE}}=E_{\mathrm{Ag/AgCl}}+0.197\mathrm{V}+0.059\mathrm{pH}$ For OER tests, the electrochemical accessibility of the working electrode was first optimized by potential cycling between 1.1 and $1.6\mathrm{V}$ at $50\mathrm{mV}{\cdot}s^{-1}$ in $1\mathrm{M}$ KOH until stable voltammogram curves were obtained. Then, the polarization curves and Tafel plots were recorded at scan rates of 5 and $0.1\mathrm{mV}{\cdot}\mathrm{s}^{-1}$ , respectively. All polarization curves were corrected with $95\%$ iR-compensation. Accurate Tafel plots of all the samples derived from the Koutecky-Levich plots were obtained according to a previous method [2]. Briefly, potentiostatic measurements were conducted with a suitable potential in increments of $20~\mathrm{mV}.$ Each potential was held for $90\:s$ . This was conducted five times with altering rotation speeds (600, 1,000, 1,500, and $2{,}000\ \mathrm{rpm}$ respectively). Thereafter, accurate Tafel plots of LDH- ${\mathrm{ND}}s@{\mathrm{C}}-$ MOF, LDH-NSs, $\mathrm{RuO}_{2}$ and C-MOF were deduced from the corresponding Koutecky-Levich plots.
