## 3 Results and discussion
The cobalt-based MOF (ZIF-67) containing the networkconstituent nanoparticles with a uniform size of $\sim200\ \mathrm{nm}$ (Figs. S2–S4 in the ESM), was synthesized as the precursors for LDH-NDs@C-MOF. A comparison of Fig. S5 with Fig. S2 in the ESM indicates that the carbon nanoparticles obtained by pyrolysis of the as-synthesized ZIF-67 at $700~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ under Ar atmosphere maintained the morphology and size of the ZIF-67 precursor. Furthermore, the resultant C-MOF was demonstrated to have a large surface area $(606.7~\mathrm{m}^{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1})$ with a high porosity $(\sim0.41~\mathrm{m^{3}{\cdot}g^{-1}})$ and uniform pore size $(\sim4.7\:\mathrm{nm})$ (Fig. S6 in the ESM), attractive as a nanoreactor for the formation of the surface-anchored LDH-NDs and the catalyst supporter [39, 40]. As schematically shown in Fig. S1 in the ESM, the LDH-NDs $\boldsymbol{@}\mathrm{C}$ -MOF was prepared through a solvothermal method in an NMP solvent containing well-dispersed C-MOF, $\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}$ and $\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}$ cations.
Due to the confinement/template synthesis, the growth of LDH nanocrystals is seriously limited, leading to LDH-NDs with a relatively small size in respect to LDH-NSs from templatefree syntheses under the same conditions without confinement (Fig. 1 and Fig. S7 in the ESM). Figures 1(a)–1(c) reproduce TEM images of the resultant LDH-NDs@C-MOF, which show that LDH-NDs with a size of $\sim4.3\:\mathrm{nm}$ homogeneously dispersed on the network-constituent carbon nanoparticles within the C-MOF. For comparison, conventional LDH-NSs were also prepared under the same condition, but without the addition of C-MOF (Fig. S7 in the ESM). The LDH-NDs@C-MOF hybrids were subjected to further studies by XRD and HRTEM. XRD patterns shown in Fig. 1(d) revealed the successful hybridization of LDH-NDs with the C-MOF. As expected, Figs. 1(e) and 1(f) show the main featured lattice space of 0.24 and $0.21{\mathrm{nm}}$ for the LDH (006) plane and Co (111) plane, respectively [37, 41]. The TEM energy dispersive spectroscopic mapping (TEM-EDS) images given in Fig. 1(g) revealed a homogenous distribution of all the elements at the relatively low magnification. In addition, Fig. 1(h) shows an $\mathrm{N}_{2}$ isotherm for the as-prepared LDH-NDs@C-MOF at $77\mathrm{K},$ from which the Brunauer–Emmett–Teller (BET) surface area was determined to be $\sim415.2\ \mathrm{m}^{2}{\cdot}\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ with a pore size of $2.5\ \mathrm{nm}$ . This BET surface area $(415.2\mathrm{m}^{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1})$ is smaller than the corresponding surface area $(606.7~\mathrm{m}^{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1})$ of the C-MOF supporter, presumably due to the integration of LDH-NDs into the C-MOF porous structure.
We further performed XAS and XPS measurements (Fig. 2 and Figs. S8–S11 in the ESM) to investigate the atomic and electronic/ chemical structures of the as-prepared LDH-NDs $@$ C-MOF. As seen in the insets of Figs. 2(a) and 2(d), the pre-edge peak intensity in both of the Ni- and Fe-K edge X-ray absorption near edge structure (XANES) for the LDH-NDs $@$ C-MOF increased compared with the corresponding peaks for Ni- and Fe-NSs, respectively, indicating the existence of defects in the LDH-NDs [36]. The extended X-ray absorption fine structure (EXAFS) analyses provide further information on the structural characters of the LDH-NDs at the atomic level. As shown in Figs. 2(b) and 2(e), the intensities of the first and second major peaks in the EXAFS-Fourier transformed (FT) moduli corresponding to the metal (M)–O and/or M–M bonds in the LDH-NDs decreased in comparison to those of the LDH NS counterparts, revealing decreased average coordination numbers of Ni and Fe cations caused by the increased number of edge defects in the LDH-NDs [2]. Besides, the defect-induced structural distortions can also be observed. Figures 2(b) and 2(e) show the bond distance shifts from $1.69\mathring{\mathrm{A}}$ for Ni–O and $1.59\mathrm{\AA}$ for Fe–O in the LDH-NS to 1.66 Å and $1.56\mathrm{~\AA~}$ for N–O and Fe–O, respectively, in the LDH-NDs. These results are also verified by EXAFS-WT analyses (Figs. 2(c) and 2(f)), which show the corresponding differences between the as-prepared
LDH-NDs@C-MOF and respective LDH-NSs. In view of the same coordination structure for the LDH-NDs and LDH-NSs, we can attribute the peak intensity decreases seen in EXAFSFT (Figs. 2(b) and 2(e)) to the presence of more (edge) defects in the LDH-NDs.
Apart from the atomic structures determined by XAS analyses discussed above, we performed XPS measurements to probe the chemical and electronic states of the LDH-NDs. As shown in Fig. S8 in the ESM, the high-resolution $\mathrm{Ni}~2\mathrm{p}_{3/2}$ XPS spectrum of the LDH-NSs shows the satellite component peaks at 858.4, 862.8 and $868.5\ \mathrm{eV},$ along with the main peak at $856.8~\mathrm{eV}$ attributable to $\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}$ cations [42]. Compared with the corresponding peaks in the LDH-NS, however, all the aforementioned Ni 2p XPS peaks in the LDH-NDs@C-MOF showed a down-shift in the binding energy (Fig. S8 in the ESM). In contrast, all the Fe $2{\tt p}_{3/2}$ XPS peaks in the LDH$\mathrm{NDs@C-MOF}$ showed an up-shift in the binding energy (Fig. S9 in the ESM), suggesting a partial-charge-transfer from Fe to Ni in the LDH-NDs $@^{\zeta}$ -MOF [43]. Similar binding energy shifts were also observed for the corresponding Ni $2\mathsf{p}_{1/2}$ and Fe $2\mathsf{p}_{1/2}$ XPS spectra (Fig. S10 in the ESM). To obtain an in-depth understanding of the charge-transfer pathway between $\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}$ and $\mathrm{Ni^{2+}}$ , we analysed the O 1s XPS peaks. As shown in Fig. S11 in the ESM, the O 1s XPS peak showed also a down-shift from $\sim532.2\:\mathrm{eV}$ for the LDH-NS to $\sim531.8\mathrm{eV}$ for the LDH- $\mathrm{\cdotNDs@C-}$ MOF, indicating that the charge transfer is from $\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}$ to $\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}$ through the bridging O to give nearly optimal binding energies for the OER intermediates [2, 22, 44].
The OER catalytic performance of the as-prepared LDH$\mathrm{NDs@C-MOF}$ , LDH-NSs and C-MOF as well as the commercial $\mathrm{RuO}_{2}$ with the same mass loading of $\sim0.2\mathrm{mg}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ were tested in the $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ -saturated 1 M KOH by using a standard threeelectrode cell. We used linear sweep voltammetry (LSV) to record polarization curves for all the samples at a scan rate of $5\mathrm{mV}{\cdot}s^{-1}$ to minimize the capacitive current [17, 45]. The anodic peaks in the potential range from $\sim1.29$ to $\sim1.41\mathrm{~V~}$ vs. RHE seen in Fig. 3(a) for the LDH-NDs@C-MOF and LDH-NSs arose from the oxidation of $\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}$ to $\mathrm{Ni}^{3+}$ . As can also be seen in Fig. 3(a) and Fig. S12 in the ESM, the LDH-NDs/C-MOF electrode showed the best LSV curve with the lowest onset potential $\ ^{\prime}E_{\mathrm{onset}}=\sim1.42\ \mathrm{V};$ ) among all the samples tested in this study, including LDH-NSs $(\sim1.48\mathrm{V})$ ), C-MOF $(1.57\mathrm{eV})$ , and even commercial $\mathrm{RuO}_{2}$ $(\sim1.43\mathrm{~V})$ . To further compare the intrinsic OER activity of C-MOF, LDH-NDs $@$ C-MOF and LDH-NSs, their LSV curves were normalized by their electrochemical active surface area (ECSA) (Fig. S13 in the ESM). Figure S14 in the ESM shows an OER activity trend of $\mathrm{LDH\mathrm{-}N D s@C\mathrm{-}M O F>L D H\mathrm{-}N S s>C\mathrm{-}M O F},$ similar to that based on the geometrical area of the electrode (Fig. 3(a)). Besides, $\boldsymbol{E_{\mathrm{onset}}}$ of the LDH- $\mathrm{NDs@C-MOF}$ is also much lower than that of the physically mixed LDH-NSs/C-MOF $(\sim1.46\mathrm{V},$ Fig. S13 in the ESM), indicating the importance of the strong interfacial interaction between the surface-anchored LDH-NDs and the C-MOF supporter. The overpotential of LDH- ${\mathrm{ND}}s@{\mathrm{C}}-$ MOF at $10\mathrm{\mA}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ $\left(E_{j=10}\right)$ is $230~\mathrm{mV}$ vs. thermodynamic OER potential $\cdot{\cal E}_{\mathrm{\small{H20/O2}}}^{0}=1.229\mathrm{~V},$ Fig. 3(a)), which is considerably smaller than those of the LDH-NSs $\mathrm{(\sim316~mV}$ ), C-MOF $\mathrm{(\sim4l0\mV)}$ , LDH-NSs/C-MOF $\mathrm{\sim271\mV},$ Fig. S15 in the ESM), recently-reported transition metal-based catalysts (Table S1 in the ESM), and even the commercial $\mathrm{RuO}_{2}$ ( $\mathrm{\sim280~mV},$ . The similar electrochemical impedance spectra (EIS) for LDH$\mathrm{NDs@C-MOF}$ and LDH-NSs/C-MOF shown in Fig. S16 in the ESM exclude possible conductivity effect on the enhanced OER activity. The outstanding electrocatalytic activity of LDHNDs@C-MOF was also confirmed by its high turnover frequency (TOF) $\left(0.9\ \mathsf{s}^{-1}\right)$ , much higher than that of LDH-NSs $(0.14~\mathsf{s}^{-1})$ .
Figure 3(b) shows Tafel plots derived from Koutecky-Levich plots for LDH-NDs@C-MOF, LDH-NSs, C-MOF and $\mathrm{RuO}_{2}$ in an $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ -saturated aqueous solution of $1\ \mathrm{M\KOH}$ . As seen in Fig. 3(b), LDH-NDs@C-MOF has a much smaller Tafel slope $(49\mathrm{mV\cdotdec^{-1}})$ than those for LDH-NSs $(83\ \mathrm{mV\cdotdec^{-1}})$ , C-MOF $(137\mathrm{mV\cdotdec^{-1}})$ , and commercial $\mathrm{RuO}_{2}$ $(65\mathrm{~mV}{\cdot}\mathrm{dec}^{-1}),$ ), implying a superb reaction kinetics for the LDH-NDs@C-MOF [46]. Compared with the disk current density, Fig. 3(c) shows a negligible current density from the Pt ring electrode, suggesting a desirable four-electron pathway for OER $\mathrm{^{\prime}4O H^{-}\rightarrow O_{2}+2H_{2}O+}$
$4\mathrm{e}^{-})$ [47]. To prove that the observed disk current was generated from water oxidation rather than side reactions, we further investigated Faradaic efficiency for OER by the rotating ring-disk electrode (RRDE) technique (Fig. S17 in the ESM). Specifically, a ring current of about $0.098\mathrm{mA}$ was detected when a given current of $0.5\mathrm{mA}$ was applied to the disk electrode for oxygen evolution, which gave a high Faradaic efficiency of $98\%$ to confirm that the observed catalytic current resulted exclusively from water oxidation. The LDH-NDs $\boldsymbol{\mathcal{Q}}\boldsymbol{\mathrm{C}}$ -MOF was further subject to the durability test. As shown in Fig. 3(d) and Fig. S18 in the ESM, LDH-NDs@C-MOF electrode showed an only $5\%$ decay after $50\mathrm{h}$ continuous electrocatalysis under a given applied potential of $1.48~\mathrm{V},$ which is better than those of the C-MOF ( $18\%$ decay), LDH-NSs ( $11\%$ decay) and commercial $\mathrm{RuO}_{2}$ ( $54\%$ decay after $^{\textrm{\scriptsize11h}}$ testing). XRD measurements were also performed on the LDH- $\mathrm{NDs}\ @\mathrm{C-MOF}$ before and after OER, suggesting no obvious structure change (Fig. S19 in the ESM).
To identify the highly active sites in the LDH-NDs $@$ C-MOF catalyst for OER, we further performed the in situ XAS spectroscopic measurements at the resting (open circuit voltage, OCV) and catalytic states. An optimized potential of $1.45\mathrm{~V~}$ was applied at catalytic state to better present the different activity behavior of LDH-NDs $@$ C-MOF and LDH-NSs. As can be seen in Figs. 4(a) and 4(c), the main Ni K-edge peaks of the LDH-NDs@C-MOF and LDH-NSs under resting state are centered at $8,350.5\ \mathrm{eV},$ which are similar to that of the standard NiO with a chemical state of $+2$ . Upon the application of a potential of $1.45\mathrm{~V~}$ (Fig. 4(a)), the main peak of LDH-NDs ${\mathcal{Q}}($ -MOF significantly shifted to a higher energy of $8{,}351.0\mathrm{eV,}$ suggesting the occurrence of Ni oxidation [7, 48]. In contrast, no shift was observed for the main Ni-K peak of LDH-NSs (Fig. 4(c)) under the same condition. Under the applied potential, the higher energy of the main Ni K-edge peak for LDH-NDs@C-MOF with respect to LDH-NSs indicates that the former has a higher oxidation state for Ni than the latter [49], leading to the higher OER activity observed for the LDH-NDs@C-MOF.
The quantitative EXAFS-FT analyses revealed that the local coordination structure for Ni in the LDH-NDs@C-MOF (Fig. 4(b)) is more distorted than that in the LDH-NSs (Fig. 4(d)). In particular, the application of $1.45\mathrm{V}$ caused the Ni–Ni coordination peaks from the LDH-NDs@C-MOF to split into two peaks (Fig. 4(b)), but not those from the LDH-NSs (Fig. 4(d), see also Fig. S20 and Table S2 in the ESM).
Furthermore, the Fe K-edge peaks of LDH-NDs@C-MOF and LDH-NSs were also investigated (Figs. S21–S24 and Table S3 in the ESM). The Fe K-edge XANES (Figs. S21 and S22 in the ESM) show no obvious shift as Fe cations with a valence of $+3$ is difficult to be further oxidized under the relatively low applied potential [16]. However, changes in the EXAFS-FT intensity can still be observed upon the application of $1.45\mathrm{~V~}$ (Figs. S23 and S24 in the ESM). As shown in Fig. S24 and Table S3 in the ESM, the Debye-Waller factor $(\sigma^{2})$ for Fe–Fe coordination shell in the LDH-NDs $\boldsymbol{@}\mathrm{C}$ -MOF shows an obvious increase from 0.009 to $0.012\mathrm{~\AA~}^{2}$ under the OCV to $1.45\mathrm{~V~}$ whereas only a very small change is seen for the LDH-NSs, implying a larger structural distortion in the LDH- ${\mathrm{ND}}s@{\mathrm{C}}.$ - MOF than in the LDH-NSs under the applied basis. These results combined with the electrocatalytic LSV curves in Fig. 3(a) reveal that the Ni and Fe sites in LDH-NDs $\boldsymbol{\mathcal{Q}}\boldsymbol{\mathrm{C}}$ -MOF are activated through the partial-charge transfer to enhance the OER catalytic activity [43, 50].
## 4 Conclusions
In summary, we have developed a novel nano-templateassisted confinement synthesis approach to a class of new LDH-NDs@C-MOF catalysts with LDH-NDs anchored onto carbon nanoparticles that formed a 3D network within a pyrolyzed porous MOF. The resultant LDH-NDs $@$ C-MOF catalysts rich with edge active sites, efficient electron paths via the 3D carbon framework, and efficient electrolyte/reactant/ product diffusion through the 3D interconnect pores within the C-MOF supporter showed superb OER performance with an overpotential as low as $\sim230~\mathrm{mV}$ at an exchange current density of $10\mathrm{\mA}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ , which is much lower than that of the large-sized LDH-NSs $(321~\mathrm{mV})$ ), pure C-MOF $\mathrm{(411~mV)}$ , and even commercial $\mathrm{RuO}_{2}$ $\mathrm{281~mV},$ ). Our combined experimental and theoretical studies, including XPS, ex situ and in situ XAS and DFT calculations revealed partial charge transfer from $\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}$ through an O bridge to $\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}$ at the edge of LDH-NDs to produce efficient active sites with optimal binding energies for OER intermediates. This, together with the efficient electron/ electrolyte/reactant/product transports intrinsically associated with the 3D MOF supporter mentioned above, significantly boosted the OER performance of the newly developed LDHNDs@C-MOF catalysts with respect to the LDH-NSs. While our finding that the catalytic activities of nanoparticle catalysts can be significantly enhanced by converting nanosheets into nanodots rich with catalytic active edge defects or vacancies is paramount important, the nano-template/confinement synthesis methodology developed in this study is general and applicable to the development of other 3D carbon supported nanoparticle catalysts for various reaction even beyond OER.
